1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 { The so-called anti-Berlin, which was long considered completely harmless but has been gaining more and more popularity in recent years due to futility of White's attempts in the Berlin Endgame. } Bc5 5. Bxc6 { A starting point of this chapter. This move, which is highly popular on the high level, is hardly an attempt to gain an opening advantage. Instead, White wants to avoid more forcing lines, which often lead to quick simplification and play a slow, positional game. } dxc6 6. Be3 { This is obviously non-critical, but was tried by some high-level players, so it's important to have some idea in mind here with Black. -KEY- } Bxe3 7. fxe3 Qe7 8. O-O Ng4 $1 { Not the only move but a nice way to regroup. } 9. Qd2 O-O $11 { The point of the 8th move is that Black wants to play ...f6 and retreat his knight via h6 to f7, with a very harmonious position and good preparation against potential d3-d4. I would prefer to play Black here. } 
 *

