[Result "*"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 { The so-called anti-Berlin, which was long considered completely harmless but has been gaining more and more popularity in recent years due to futility of White's attempts in the Berlin Endgame. } Bc5 5. Bxc6 { A starting point of this chapter. This move, which is highly popular on the high level, is hardly an attempt to gain an opening advantage. Instead, White wants to avoid more forcing lines, which often lead to quick simplification and play a slow, positional game. } dxc6 6. Nbd2 { This is not merely a different move order than 6.0-0 or 6.h3. By not committing himself on the kingside, White keeps an option of building an aggressive setup with Nc4, Qe2, h4-h5, Bd2 and 0-0-0 after the usual ...Nd7 and ...0-0. Of course, Black has his ways to counter this idea, but he has to react in a very nuanced way and be constantly careful about White's threats on the kingside. This is why I recommend a different, much simpler and safer approach for Black here. -KEY- } Be6 { Stopping Nc4 but } ( 6... Nd7 { is also OK. } ) 
7. Nb3 Bb6 8. Nxe5 { This move leads to pretty forced simplifications. } Bxb3 9. axb3 Qd4 10. Ng4 { All forced. } Nxg4 11. Qxg4 Qxf2+ 12. Kd1 O-O { Black is fine, e.g. } 13. Ra4 Rae8 14. Bd2 f5 15. exf5 Qxf5 $11 
